 "drivingQuestion": "Why should the sinner's meal offering precede when the voluntary one requires oil and frankincense?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "The mishna's order stands because the sinner's offering atones",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "zevachim.90a.7",
   "daf": "90a",
   "sentenceId": 6,
   "he": "מִנְחַת חוֹטֵא כּוּ׳. אַדְּרַבָּה – מִנְחַת נְדָבָה קוֹדֶמֶת, שֶׁכֵּן טְעוּנָה שֶׁמֶן וּלְבוֹנָה! אֲפִילּוּ הָכִי, מִנְחַת חוֹטֵא הַבָּאָה עַל חֵטְא עֲדִיפָא, דִּמְכַפֶּרֶת.",
   "en": "The mishna further teaches: «The meal offering of a sinner» precedes a voluntary meal offering. The Gemara challenges: «On the contrary, a voluntary meal offering» should «precede» the meal offering of a sinner, «as it requires oil and frankincense.» The Gemara explains: «Even so, the meal offering of a sinner, which comes due to a sin,» is of «greater» importance, «as» it «effects atonement.»"
  }
